{"answer":"\\((x^2-4)/(x-2)=x+2\\) for \\(x\\ne2\\). The original expression has domain \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{2\\}\\). The simplified expression \\(x+2\\) is not equivalent to the original at every real number: at \\(x=2\\), it equals \\(4\\), while the original quotient is undefined. They are equivalent on the original domain only.","approaches":[{"description":"Factor the numerator as a difference of squares, then cancel the common nonzero factor on the original domain.","id":"factor-cancel","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This directly establishes the simplification while retaining the excluded value."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question asks about \"For real x where the expression is defined\" for the displayed quotient.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"original-expression","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The expression under consideration is \\((x^2-4)/(x-2)\\) for real \\(x\\) where it is defined.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in factorization and cancellation steps.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["original-expression"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"simplification","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every real \\(x\\ne2\\), \\((x^2-4)/(x-2)=x+2\\).","step_ids":["factor-numerator","cancel-factor"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from the denominator condition and direct evaluation of the simplified expression.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["original-expression","simplification"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"domain-and-nonequivalence","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The original domain is \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{2\\}\\), and the original and simplified expressions are not equivalent at every real number.","step_ids":["domain","compare-at-two"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"factor-numerator","justification":"Expanding \\((x-2)(x+2)\\) gives \\(x^2-4\\).","statement":"Rewrite the numerator as \\(x^2-4=(x-2)(x+2)\\)."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["factor-numerator"],"id":"domain","justification":"A quotient is defined only when its denominator is nonzero; thus \\(x-2\\ne0\\), equivalently \\(x\\ne2\\).","statement":"The original expression is defined exactly for real \\(x\\ne2\\)."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["factor-numerator","domain"],"id":"cancel-factor","justification":"For \\(x\\ne2\\), the factor \\(x-2\\) is nonzero, so \\(((x-2)(x+2))/(x-2)=x+2\\).","statement":"On the original domain, cancel \\(x-2\\) to obtain \\(x+2\\)."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["domain","cancel-factor"],"id":"compare-at-two","justification":"At \\(x=2\\), the original denominator is zero and the quotient is undefined, whereas \\(x+2=4\\).","statement":"The simplified expression has a value at \\(x=2\\), but the original expression does not."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
